The question asks whether PostgreSQL uses SAVEPOINT to roll back part of the statements within a transaction.

This is true. In PostgreSQL, SAVEPOINT allows you to create a named point within a transaction to which you can later roll back. This means you can undo part of the statements within a transaction without rolling back the entire transaction.

For example:
```sql
BEGIN;
INSERT ...;
SAVEPOINT s1;
INSERT ...; -- fails
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT s1;
-- transaction continues with the first INSERT preserved
COMMIT;
```

